      ":question/id": "09ed8d3f-e8c4-5182-b1db-e5d98c04df75",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation $y' + 4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation $y' + 2 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y^{2} = x \\cdot y^{3}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation $y' + 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=3 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad q(x)=4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x},\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select $y' + 3 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{4 \\cdot \\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$."
    },
