      ":question/id": "1ca4778d-d0ee-5be1-91bb-deb87c2a6870",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following equations is a Bernoulli differential equation?\n\nWork on intervals where $x>0$ and $y>0$, and where the displayed coefficients are defined.\n\nSelect all that apply. If none apply, leave all boxes unchecked.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation $y' - y^{2} \\cdot e^{x} = 5 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation $y^{3}y' + 2 \\cdot y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y^{3}$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + 2 \\cdot y \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = \\frac{2}{y^{\\frac{5}{2}}}\n$$\n\n In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=2 \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)},\\quad q(x)=2,\\quad n=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n$$\n\n- Equation $yy' + 3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)} = \\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. Dividing by $y$, we get\n\n$$\ny' + \\frac{3 \\cdot x \\cdot \\sin{\\left(y \\right)}}{y} = \\frac{\\cos^{2}{\\left(y \\right)}}{y}\n$$\n\n The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\nTherefore, select $y^{3}y' + 2 \\cdot y^{4} \\cdot \\log{\\left(x \\right)} = 2 \\cdot \\sqrt{y}$."
    },
