      ":question/worked-solution": "A Bernoulli differential equation has the form\n\n$$\ny'+p(x)y=q(x)y^n.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine the given options. Work on the stated positive domain, so dividing by powers of $y$ is valid.\n\n- Equation $y' + \\sqrt{x} \\cdot y = 2 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot \\log{\\left(y \\right)}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains a function of $y$ that is not a single power of $y$.\n\n- Equation $y' + x^{2} \\cdot y^{2} = 4 \\cdot x \\cdot y^{3}$ is *not* a Bernoulli differential equation. The normalized equation contains two different powers of $y$, neither with exponent 1.\n\n- Equation $y' + 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$ is a Bernoulli differential equation. In this case, we have\n\n$$\np(x)=5 \\cdot x^{2},\\quad q(x)=\\sqrt{x},\\quad n=- \\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, select $y' + 5 \\cdot x^{2} \\cdot y = \\frac{\\sqrt{x}}{\\sqrt{y}}$."
    },
